<!-- CROSSWALK -->
<section class="wrap chapter" id="crosswalk" aria-labelledby="h-cw">
  <div class="lens-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">The crosswalk</p>
    <h2 id="h-cw">I map everything before merging anything</h2>
    <p>A starting point I've often met: data guidance and AI guidance on two sites, each structured its own way.</p>
    <div class="why"><span class="lab">Why it matters · an illustrative example</span><p>Someone at Fernlow needs the rule for using AI with rider data. They check the data office's site, then the AI group's site, then ask a colleague. One front door means one place to start.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="scrolly">
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step" data-step="0" id="s-cw-0"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">Two sites</p>
        <h3>People have to know which site holds the answer</h3>
        <p>At Fernlow, the data office's site is organized by team. The AI group's site is a tools list and an FAQ.</p>
        <p><span class="cl inf">inferred</span>In my experience, a split like this also hides how closely data rules and AI rules depend on each other.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="1" id="s-cw-1"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">Current state</p>
        <h3>Every page and section, mapped</h3>
        <p>Before changing anything, I map every page and section on both sites as they stand.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="2" id="s-cw-2"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">The crosswalk</p>
        <h3>One shared set of categories, written as questions</h3>
        <p>Every piece of content on both sites is mapped to the same categories. I write them as the questions people bring: where do I start, what are the rules, is my use safe, how do I learn it, where's the data, and who can help.</p>
        <p data-aud="gen">A crosswalk is a table that maps every old page to its place in the new structure.</p>
        <p data-aud="pro">In crosswalk terms, the dashed lines are one-to-many splits and the shaded categories are many-to-one merges.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="3" id="s-cw-3"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">The merge plan</p>
        <h3>I decide what to merge once I know what exists</h3>
        <p>Mapping first is how I make every merge a decision, so nothing is lost or duplicated by accident when the two sites come together.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="graphic">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="sec dim" data-on="0,1">
          <span class="lab">Current state · invented sections</span>
          <div class="oldsites s0"></div>
          <div class="chips" style="margin-top:8px">
            <span class="chip flag tag" data-on="0">Which site has the answer?</span>
            <span class="chip ink tag" data-on="1">every page and section mapped</span>
          </div>
          <p class="caption tag" data-on="0"><span class="halfkey">half</span> where each half of the find-it answer lived</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sec dim" data-on="2,3">
          <span class="lab">Crosswalk · the same sections, mapped to the shared categories</span>
          <svg class="cm s2" viewBox="0 0 360 320" width="100%" role="group" aria-label="Crossmap from the invented old sections to six shared categories written as questions. Each box can be focused to trace its links."></svg>
          <div class="cmkey"><span><i class="k-split"></i>one section split across categories</span><span><i class="k-many"></i>a category gathering several sections</span><span class="dim" data-on="3"><i class="k-merge"></i>gathers from both sites: a merge decision</span></div>
          <p class="caption">Left: the invented sections from above. Right: the shared categories, written as questions. Hover, focus or tap a box to trace it.</p>
          <ul class="sr cmlist"></ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- STRUCTURE -->
<section class="wrap chapter" id="structure" aria-labelledby="h-st">
  <div class="lens-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">The structure</p>
    <h2 id="h-st">Data and AI, side by side</h2>
    <p>The structure I reached for here puts one homepage above paired data and AI pages. It's one shape among several; a single merged taxonomy is another. Pairs keep two distinct disciplines distinct.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scrolly">
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step" data-step="0" id="s-st-0"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">The homepage</p>
        <h3>Quick links for the questions people bring</h3>
        <p>One homepage, with a quick link for each question and a short list of what's new.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="1" id="s-st-1"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">Pairs</p>
        <h3>Connected, and kept distinct</h3>
        <p>Rules, safe use and skills each get a data page and an AI page side by side. <span class="cl inf">inferred</span>The pairing shows how the two connect without merging two distinct disciplines.</p>
        <p>Learning paths can sit underneath, drawing on both.</p>
        <p data-aud="pro">In IA terms: two parallel hierarchies, cross-linked level by level, instead of one merged taxonomy.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="2" id="s-st-2"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">Guardrails with skills</p>
        <h3>I put the guardrails next to the skills</h3>
        <p>A page that teaches a skill links to its safe-use page at the same level, so the guardrails arrive with the skill instead of after it.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="3" id="s-st-3"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">Reference lists</p>
        <h3>Spreadsheets become governed lists</h3>
        <p>Reference content, such as the list of data domains<span class="gloss" data-aud="gen"> (the major subject areas of an organization's data, such as riders, routes or fares)</span>, moves out of spreadsheets into a list with an owner for every row, so it can be governed and searched.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="4" id="s-st-4"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">Design review</p>
        <h3>Comparing approaches with AI</h3>
        <p>When I compare design approaches with AI, I keep what the evidence shows separate from what I recommend.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="graphic">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="sec dim" data-on="0,1,2">
          <div class="panel-head"><span class="lab">Front door · invented example</span><span class="chip">schematic</span></div>
